.TH XPAR 1 "2026-08-26" "xpar 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
xpar \- error and erasure correction for guarding data integrity
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xpar
.I verb
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B xpar
.I set
.RS
same as
.B xpar verify
.IR set .
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B xpar
creates recovery volumes for files and directories. These volumes can restore
data damaged by bad sectors, bit flips, truncation, media rot, or transmission
failures.
.SH VERBS
Any unambiguous verb prefix is accepted. An exact match takes precedence.
.TP
.B create
Build a redundancy set from the input paths. Directories require
.BR \-R .
When rerun over a protected tree, xpar skips and reports its own volumes.
.TP
.B verify
Check data integrity without modifying the set. Armoured plaintext is staged
securely and removed on exit. Exit status distinguishes clean, repairable, and
unrepairable data. See
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.TP
.B repair
Verify and repair errors or erasures.
.B \-\-in\-place
also recreates missing manifest objects, restores recorded metadata, rebuilds
damaged packet volumes from replicas, and fixes misplaced or mis-sized split
volumes.
.TP
.B scrub
Read all data and recovery volumes and report corrected symbols per codeword.
With
.B \-\-deep
recompute recovery data from the protected data.
.TP
.B extract
Reconstruct data from a split or armoured set (see
.BR \-\-layout ).
A sidecar set needs no extraction. Armoured sets correct inner-code damage
while reading. Unrecoverable data exits 2.
.TP
.B recover
Regenerate one volume of a set from the others.
.TP
.B addrecovery
Add recovery slices to an existing generation.
.TP
.B add
Append a generation protecting new or changed files.
.TP
.B consolidate
Re-encode a generation chain as one generation. Without
.B \-r
it inherits the chain's widest redundancy.
.TP
.B prune
Remove older generations from a chain, keeping any generation that a
surviving entry still depends on unless
.B \-f
is given.
.PP
Both exit 2 rather than rewrite a chain containing an unreadable generation.
.TP
.B list
Print the manifest of the set.
.TP
.B info
Print geometry, codec, plan, redundancy, creator, and comments. For
.BR \-\-layout=armoured ,
report the actual on-disk armour overhead.
.B \-\-deps
adds the dependency table used by
.BR prune .
Its
.B superseded/R
column shows recovery consumed by superseded slices.
.TP
.B explain
Print the exact
.BR dd (1)
commands needed to extract the armoured region without xpar or correction.
For
.BR \-\-layout=armoured ;
this is protected data. For sidecar and split sets it is critical metadata.
.TP
.B undo
Replay an in-place repair journal after a crash interrupted an operation.
.TP
.B recover\-prologue
Brute-force the parameters of a destroyed armoured prologue.
.TP
.B benchmark
Measure xpar's low-level SIMD kernels.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Global options must follow the verb.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Repeatable.
.B \-vv
reports per-slice detail.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Diagnostics only.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Overwrite and permit guarded operations.
.TP
.BI \-j " N" "\fR, \fP" \-\-jobs= N
Worker threads. Default is one per core.
.TP
.BI \-m " SIZE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-memory= SIZE
Planner working-set limit. If no plan fits, xpar reports the required size.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write JSON Lines to standard output and human-readable output to standard
error.
.TP
.BR \-\-progress ", " \-\-no\-progress
Enable or disable progress on standard error. Without either option, progress
appears only on a terminal and at most once per second.
.TP
.BI \-\-color= WHEN
.IR auto " (default), " always ", " never .
.TP
.B \-\-reproducible
Omit source timestamps and the creator version for reproducible output.
Permissions, attributes, and links remain unless changed with
.BR \-\-preserve .
Setting
.B SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
also enables this option. Host names are never recorded.
.TP
.BI \-\-simd= TIER
Force a SIMD tier. The default is
.IR auto .
Unsupported tiers are refused.
.TP
.BI \-\-auth\-key= FILE
Read the authentication key from
.IR FILE .
Empty files are rejected. Authentication does not encrypt the set. Every verb
that opens it requires the key. See
.BR SECURITY .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display usage information.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
Sizes take
.BR K ", " M ", " G ", " T
(1024-based) or
.BR KB ", " MB ", " GB ", " TB
(1000-based); an unsuffixed number is bytes.
.SH CREATE OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " BASE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-output= BASE
Base name for output volumes.
.TP
.BI \-r " SPEC" "\fR, \fP" \-\-recovery= SPEC
Recovery count, percentage, size, or multiple. See
.BR REDUNDANCY .
Omitting it makes
.B create
use five percent.
.B add
and
.B consolidate
inherit existing redundancy and warn if protection decreases.
.TP
.BI \-\-min\-recovery= N
Minimum derived recovery-slice count.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-recovery= SPEC
Reserve recovery-axis space so
.B addrecovery
can add slices later.
.TP
.BI \-s " SIZE" "\fR, \fP" \-\-slice\-size= SIZE
Set slice size. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-b .
.TP
.BI \-b " N" "\fR, \fP" \-\-slices= N
Plan for this many data slices. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-s .
.TP
.BI \-\-cell= SIZE
Set cell size Y, the erasure unit. It must be a multiple of 64 bytes and at
least 4096. Smaller cells localize damage but require more checksums. The
default is min(64 KiB, Z). Slices below 4096 bytes use the whole slice. A
.B \-\-cell
larger than the slice is refused.
.TP
.BI \-\-layout= WHICH
.IR sidecar " (default), " split ", or " armoured .
.B add
takes the existing chain's layout and cannot change it.
.B consolidate
may select a new layout.
.TP
.BI \-\-codec= WHICH
.IR auto " (default), " fft ", or " matrix .
.TP
.BI \-\-field= W
.IR auto " (default), 8, or 16-bit outer field."
.TP
.BI \-\-align= WHICH
.IR none " (default), " slice ", or " 1k .
.TP
.BI \-\-slice\-tag= W
.IR none ", 8 (default), or 16 bytes."
.TP
.BI \-\-armour= WHICH
.IR none ", " metadata ", or " all .
.I metadata
is the default for
.B \-\-layout=sidecar
and
.BR \-\-layout=split ,
and protects critical metadata.
.I all
also protects slice tables and recovery slices. The default GF(2^8)
RS(255,223) code adds about 14 percent to recovery volumes, or about 1.5
percent of protected data at
.BR \-r " 10%."
Large recovery slices may use GF(2^16) with less than 2 percent overhead.
.B \-\-armour\-field
forces the field. Reading an
.B all
set materialises each wrapped packet, so it needs about as much memory as
the recovery data it holds.
.I all
is the default and the only level for
.BR \-\-layout=armoured ,
which protects the whole archive. Bare split data volumes remain raw.
.TP
.BI \-\-armour\-field= W
.IR auto " (default), 8, or 16-bit inner field."
.TP
.BI \-\-armour\-t= N
Correct up to
.I N
symbols per inner-code codeword. Use four or more.
.B \-\-armour\-t=1
may mistake two-symbol damage for a one-symbol error. The outer checksum catches
the failed repair. A
.I N
that makes a codeword exceed four times its plaintext is refused.
.TP
.BI \-\-armour\-pct= P
Set inner-code overhead relative to the protected data, so that 2t/k is
at most
.IR P .
Greater than zero and at most 50 percent. Excludes
.BR \-\-armour\-t .
.TP
.BI \-\-burst= SIZE
Choose interleave depth for this burst size. Excludes
.BR \-\-depth .
.TP
.BI \-\-depth= D
Set interleave depth. The default is 1. A depth exceeding
.B \-m
is refused with the required memory and largest feasible value.
.B \-\-burst
rounds up to meet the requested tolerance.
.TP
.BI \-\-volumes= WHICH
.IR ladder " (default), " equal ", or a positive count."
.TP
.BI \-\-dedup= WHICH
.IR none ", " file " (default), or " chunk .
.TP
.BI \-\-dedup\-chunk= SIZE
Chunk size for chunk deduplication.
.TP
.BI \-\-dedup\-memory= SIZE
Memory limit for the fingerprint index used by
.B \-\-dedup=chunk
only. Exceeding it is an error.
.TP
.BI \-\-dedup\-max\-refs= N
Maximum aliases of one stored range. Zero, the default, does not limit
them.
.TP
.BI \-\-preserve= LIST
Metadata to record. Tokens are
.BR mtime , atime , ctime , btime , times , mode , setid , attrs , owner ,
.BR xattr , xattr-all , links , all , none .
A bare list replaces the default; prefixes
.B +
and
.B \-
add and remove tokens. The default is
.BR mtime,mode,attrs,links .
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-recurse
Descend into directories.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude= GLOB
Exclude matching paths; repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-include= GLOB
Repeatable. Supplying it at all restricts the selection to matching
paths; it does not re-admit paths that
.B \-\-exclude
removed.
.TP
.B \-\-follow\-symlinks
Store target content rather than the symbolic link.
.TP
.BI \-\-base= DIR
Store names relative to
.IR DIR ,
which must be the input or its parent. Relative and absolute spellings are
accepted, so
.I DIR
may be absolute when inputs are not.
.TP
.B \-\-labels
Write a label file per volume. Only
.B \-\-layout=split
has data volumes to label, so the other layouts refuse it.
.TP
.B \-\-auth\-only
Omit public CRC and whole-file hashes from an authenticated set. It works
at any set size.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify\-after
Skip the post-write read-back pass. Manifest validation still occurs before
writing.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Refuse names the format cannot store. Without this option, xpar reports and
skips them.
.TP
.B \-\-spool
Stage a pipe in a temporary file beside the output.
.TP
.BI \-\-spool\-dir= DIR
Stage it under
.I DIR
instead, which implies
.BR \-\-spool .
.TP
.BI \-\-stdin\-name= PATH
Manifest path for a lone standard-input argument.
.PP
.B add
and
.B consolidate
also accept the create options.
.SH OTHER VERB OPTIONS
.TP
.BR "verify \-\-fast"
Check tags without the algebraic pass.
.TP
.BR "verify \-\-strong"
Check the BLAKE3 slice tags, not just the CRC32C slice checksums.
Entry hashes are checked whenever
.B \-\-fast
is absent, with or without this.
.B verify
warns about invalid recovery slices and slices superseded by later
generations. Both reduce available recovery but do not make intact data
damaged.
.TP
.BI "verify \-\-resync=" WHICH ", " "repair \-\-resync=" WHICH
.IR off ", " auto " (default), or " always .
.TP
.BI "verify \-\-resync\-step=" N ", " "repair \-\-resync\-step=" N
Sample every
.I Nth
candidate offset.
.TP
.BI "verify \-\-resync\-window=" SIZE ", " "repair \-\-resync\-window=" SIZE
Search this far on either side of an expected offset.
.TP
.BR "verify \-\-resync\-exhaustive" ", " "repair \-\-resync\-exhaustive"
Confirm every misplaced-data candidate. Recovering from deleted bytes often
needs
.B \-\-resync=always
with this option.
.TP
.BI \-\-scan= DIR
Also search
.I DIR
for missing index and recovery volumes. Every verb that opens a set accepts it.
.BR verify ", " repair ", " scrub ", " extract ", " recover ", "
.BR addrecovery ", " add ", " consolidate ", " prune ", " list ", "
.BR info " and " explain .
Local volumes take precedence. Protected entries still resolve beside the set,
not under
.IR DIR .
.TP
.BR "repair \-\-in\-place" ", " "repair \-\-to=DIR"
Rewrite originals, or write a repaired tree under
.IR DIR .
.TP
.BR "repair \-\-backup" ", " "repair \-\-paranoid"
Keep damaged originals, or re-read and re-verify every write.
.TP
.BR "repair \-\-keep\-journal" ", " "repair \-\-no\-journal"
Keep the journal after success, or disable it. These exclude each other.
The journal is created with mode 0600 and is never written through a
symbolic link planted under its name.
It records changed bytes, lengths, objects, hard links, and trimmed volume
tails. Rebuilt redundancy and restored metadata are not rollback records.
.TP
.B "repair \-\-replace\-journal"
Replace an existing undo journal. Without this option, the journal stops the
repair so the earlier operation can still be undone. Invalid journals are also
retained because xpar cannot tell whether protected writes began.
.TP
.BR "repair \-\-dry\-run" ", " "repair \-\-exit\-on\-change"
Report without writing, or exit 1 when data changed. Combined, they exit 1
when writes would occur and 0 otherwise.
.TP
.BI "repair \-\-require=" LIST
Turn the named metadata degradations into errors, as under extract.
.TP
.BR "scrub \-\-deep" ", " "scrub \-\-rewrite" ", " "scrub \-\-rebuild\-cells"
Re-encode the recovery data from the protected data and compare it,
write corrections back, or rebuild per-cell CRCs.
.TP
.BI "extract \-\-to=" DIR
Extract below
.IR DIR .
.TP
.B "extract \-\-stdout"
Write the one entry of a single-entry set to standard output.
.TP
.BI "extract \-\-preserve=" LIST
Apply the metadata classes described under create.
.TP
.BI "extract \-\-owner\-map=" WHICH
.IR name " (default) or " numeric .
.TP
.BI "extract \-\-require=" LIST
Turn the named metadata degradations into errors.
.TP
.B "extract \-\-strict\-names"
Apply the Windows and DOS naming rules whatever the host is.
.TP
.BI "recover \-\-volume=" WHICH
Regenerate the named or numbered volume.
.TP
.BI "recover \-\-to=" DIR
Write the recovered volume below
.IR DIR .
.TP
.BI "addrecovery \-r " SPEC
Add recovery slices within the reserved axis.
.TP
.BI "add \-\-rescan=" WHICH
.IR stat " (default), " hash ", or " none .
Under
.I hash
an entry with unchanged content inherits its bytes, so metadata-only changes
add no stream data.
.I stat
may re-store such an entry.
.TP
.BR "add \-\-verify\-unchanged" ", " "add \-\-allow\-missing"
Alias for
.BR \-\-rescan=hash ,
or continue past a vanished entry.
.TP
.BI "add \-\-dedup\-scope=" WHICH
.IR generation " (default) or " chain .
.TP
.BR "consolidate \-\-replace" ", " "consolidate \-\-dry\-run"
Remove collapsed generations, or report without writing.
.TP
.BI "prune \-\-before=" G
Remove every generation before
.IR G .
.TP
.BI "prune \-\-generation=" G
Remove generation
.IR G ;
repeatable.
.TP
.B "prune \-\-dry\-run"
Report without modifying the chain.
.TP
.BR "list \-\-links" ", " "list \-\-dedup"
Group link aliases, or show extents and reference counts.
.TP
.B "info \-\-deps"
Show per-generation dependencies used by
.BR prune .
.TP
.B "benchmark \-\-tiers"
List and time every runnable kernel tier after differential tests.
.PP
.B \-\-generation=G
selects by generation number or set-ID prefix where accepted.
.B \-\-chain
processes every generation oldest first where accepted.
Without
.BR \-\-generation ,
.BR verify ,
.B repair
and
.B scrub
already process multi-generation sets this way. This is required to check
inherited data. Superseded ancestor slices consume recovery and are reported as
such. With
.BR \-\-generation ,
only the selected generation is processed.
.SH LAYOUTS
.TP
.B sidecar
Original files remain external. The index is
.IB base .xpa;
recovery volumes contain packets. Originals have outer-code protection but
are not inner-armoured. The inner code protects critical metadata, plus slice
tables and recovery slices under
.BR \-\-armour=all .
.TP
.B split
Bare
.IB base .d NN
volumes hold consecutive set-stream ranges with no header, trailer, or tail
padding. Concatenating them numerically yields the exact stream. Index and
recovery volumes contain packets. As with sidecar sets,
.B \-\-armour=all
also protects slice tables and recovery slices. Bare data volumes remain raw.
.TP
.B armoured
One
.IB base .xpa
contains three protected prologue copies and an inner-coded packet stream
holding manifest, data, and recovery. Use
.B extract
rather than concatenation.
.PP
Readers identify volumes and generations by stored IDs and content tags, not
file names.
.SH REDUNDANCY
.B \-r
accepts four forms.
.TP
.B 100
a slice count.
.TP
.B 15.7%
a percentage of the protected data.
.TP
.B 2.5M
an absolute number of recovery bytes.
.TP
.B 1x
a multiple of the data, so
.B \-r 1x
is mirroring.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. For
.BR verify ,
no damage.
.TP
.B 1
.BR verify
damage found and repairable.
.BR "repair \-\-exit\-on\-change"
something was repaired, or, under
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
would have been.
.TP
.B 2
Damage exceeds the available recovery. This includes failed verification,
repair, extraction, volume recovery, and prologue recovery.
.TP
.B 3
Set not found, not an xpar set, or an unsupported format version.
.TP
.B 4
Usage error, including invalid geometry or incompatible options.
.TP
.B 5
I/O error.
.TP
.B 6
Missing key, wrong key, or MAC mismatch.
.TP
.B 7
No feasible plan under
.BR \-m ,
or out of memory.
.TP
.B 8
Internal error. This is a bug.
.PP
.B repair
without
.B \-\-exit\-on\-change
exits 0 whether or not it changed data.
.B verify
distinguishes clean, repairable, and unrepairable data.
.SH EXAMPLES
Protect a file at 10 percent redundancy, then check it.
.PP
.RS
.nf
xpar create \-r 10% \-o backup movie.mkv
xpar verify backup.xpa
.fi
.RE
.PP
Repair in place with an undo journal.
.PP
.RS
.nf
xpar repair \-\-in\-place backup.xpa
.fi
.RE
.PP
Protect a directory tree and deduplicate identical files.
.PP
.RS
.nf
xpar create \-r 15% \-\-dedup=file \-o photos \-R ~/Pictures
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check media health and report corrected symbols.
.PP
.RS
.nf
xpar scrub photos.xpa
.fi
.RE
.PP
Print a recovery recipe that does not require xpar.
.PP
.RS
.nf
xpar explain backup.xpa
.fi
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH
If present, implies
.BR \-\-reproducible .
The value is not embedded in the set. Unless explicitly requested with
.BR \-\-preserve ,
source timestamps and ownership are omitted, and the creator has no version.
Host names are never stored.
.TP
.BR TMPDIR ", " TMP ", " TEMP
The first set variable selects where
.B verify
stages the plaintext of an armoured archive too large for
.BR \-m .
Otherwise xpar uses the host temporary directory, then the archive directory.
The mode 0600 stage is removed on exit.
.SH SECURITY
Input volumes and manifests are untrusted. xpar validates record lengths,
bounds allocations by input size, and searches for valid replicas when a packet
is invalid.
.PP
Extraction rejects empty and absolute names, drive and UNC names,
.B .
and
.B ..
components, control bytes, duplicate paths, and platform-invalid components.
It does not follow existing symlinks while constructing output paths. Hard-link
targets must be regular manifest entries. Privileged metadata and links are
applied only when requested. Unsupported restoration is reported, and
.B \-\-require
makes it fatal.
.PP
.B \-\-auth\-key
authenticates metadata and content but provides neither encryption nor
freshness.
An authenticated archive requires the key for every verb, including
.BR explain ;
a missing or wrong key exits 6 everywhere.
.PP
Authentication only protects a set when the verifier always supplies the
key. An attacker without it cannot forge a MAC, but can remove authentication
and retag the set. A keyless
.B verify
may then accept the unauthenticated set. The calling script must require the
key.
.PP
Public hashes remain useful without
.BR \-\-auth\-only ,
but require packet authentication to be trusted.
.SH VERSION 1 ARCHIVES
Version 2.0 cannot read version 1 archives. To migrate, decode with xpar 1.x
and protect the result with this version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IB base .xpa
The index volume holding the manifest, slice tables, and volume layout.
.TP
.IB base .v NN + MM .xpa
A recovery volume holding
.I MM
recovery slices, the first of which covers the stream from slice
.IR NN .
The number is a slice index, not a Reed-Solomon exponent. Numbers use at least
two digits.
.TP
.IB base .d NN
A bare split-layout data volume. Numeric order reconstructs the set stream.
.TP
.IB base .g GGG .xpa
The index volume for generation
.I GGG
after generation zero.
.TP
.IB base .g GGG .v NN + MM .xpa
A recovery volume owned by generation
.IR GGG .
.TP
.IB base .g GGG .d NN
A split data volume owned by generation
.IR GGG .
.TP
.IB base .d NN .xpa
Optional volume label written beside each bare data volume by
.BR \-\-labels .
.TP
.IB base .xparundo
An in-place repair journal. Its presence indicates an interrupted repair.
.B xpar undo
replays it. Invalid journals are reported and retained.
.TP
.IB base .xparmaint
A maintenance journal left by an interrupted
.B consolidate \-\-replace
or
.BR prune .
.B xpar repair
completes or rolls back the update. Invalid journals are retained.
